{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high vs. realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 1–3 €/t near-term, widening to ~5–10 €/t beyond ~2–3 weeks when trend is bearish).",
    "Horizon-dependent overestimation: error grows with forecast horizon; the back half of the 30d path is most inflated. Use an increasing negative correction with lead time (e.g., linear ramp-down by horizon).",
    "Downtrend lag / insufficient momentum: model reacts too slowly to sharp sell-offs and then keeps prices elevated. Add a trend-following adjustment: if last 10–15d slope is negative, subtract extra proportional to recent decline rate.",
    "Rebound overshoot: after local dips, the model projects strong rebounds that do not materialize (systematic overshoot of recoveries). Damp positive day-to-day changes in the forecast (cap daily increases or shrink forecast deviations toward a lower baseline).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: model mean-reverts upward toward a too-high anchor. Re-anchor mean reversion to a lower reference (e.g., recent 5–10d average) and strengthen pull toward the prevailing trend rather than long-run level.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: model captures declines better early on but overstates upside volatility later. Apply asymmetric scaling—reduce forecast amplitude above the current spot/near-term mean more than below it."
  ]
}
